' nomo 1

' # LLC resonant converter — first-harmonic design
'
' A worked design for a half-bridge LLC stage, carried out the way the
' textbooks do it: replace the square wave by its first harmonic, replace the
' rectifier and its load by the equivalent resistance they present at that
' frequency, and what is left is a linear network that can be written down.
' Steigerwald's 1988 comparison of resonant topologies is where the method comes
' from, and every converter application note since repeats it.
'
' It is here because it is the shape a real engineering worksheet has: a page of
' stated inputs, a chain of definitions that never mutates anything, a complex
' impedance, three gain curves that have to be compared against each other, and
' a verdict at the end that says whether the design meets the specification.
' image tank 520x260

' The specification.
V_bus = 400 V
V_out = 48 V
P_out = 1000 W
f_sw_min = 60 kHz
f_sw_max = 200 kHz

' The bridge drives a square wave between 0 and V_bus. Only its first harmonic
' is kept: peak 4/π of the half-swing, and the network sees the rms of that.
V_sq_pk = 4/π*(V_bus/2)
V_sq_rms = V_sq_pk/sqrt(2)

' The rectifier and the output load together look resistive to that harmonic.
' The 8/π² is the same 4/π twice over — once for the voltage the bridge applies
' and once for the current the rectifier draws.
R_load = V_out^2/P_out
I_out = P_out/V_out
n = V_bus/V_out
R_ac = 8/π^2*n^2*R_load

' The tank is chosen from three numbers: where it resonates, how heavily it is
' damped at full load, and how much magnetising inductance sits across it.
f0 = 100 kHz
Q_nom = 0.45
Ln = 4

' Everything else follows. Z0 is the characteristic impedance the chosen Q asks
' for at this load; the resonant frequency then fixes the two components.
Z0 = Q_nom*R_ac
T0 = 1/(2*π*f0)
Lr = Z0*T0 -> µH
Cr = T0/Z0 -> nF
Lm = Ln*Lr -> µH

' Worth checking that the parts that came out reproduce the numbers they were
' derived from, since a slip in the algebra above would not show up anywhere
' else on the page.
f0_check = 1/(2*π*sqrt(Lr*Cr)) -> kHz
Q_check = sqrt(Lr/Cr)/R_ac

' The second resonance, with the magnetising inductance in series. The converter
' cannot be operated below it, so it is the floor on the sweep.
f_min_res = 1/(2*π*sqrt(Cr*(Lr + Lm))) -> kHz

' The tank impedance seen by the bridge: Lr and Cr in series with Lm in
' parallel with the reflected load. Complex, because the phase is the whole
' point — the bridge has to see an inductive load to switch softly.
fn X_Lr(f) = 2*π*f*Lr
fn X_Lm(f) = 2*π*f*Lm
fn X_Cr(f) = 1/(2*π*f*Cr)
fn Z_par(f) = X_Lm(f)*i*R_ac/(X_Lm(f)*i + R_ac)
fn Z_in(f) = X_Lr(f)*i - X_Cr(f)*i + Z_par(f)

' Taken apart the three ways a worksheet asks for.
Z_at_f0 = Z_in(f0) -> ohm
R_in = Re(Z_at_f0)
X_in = Im(Z_at_f0)
mag = abs(Z_at_f0) -> ohm
phase = arg(Z_at_f0)*180/π

' Positive is inductive, which is the half of the plane the bridge must stay in.
fn phase_deg(f) = arg(Z_in(f))*180/π
soft_switching = phase_deg(f_sw_min) > 0

' The gain, normalised to the resonant frequency. This is the expression the
' whole first-harmonic method exists to produce.
fn gain(f, Q) = (f/f0)^2*(Ln - 1)/sqrt((Ln*(f/f0)^2 - 1)^2 + (f/f0)^2*((f/f0)^2 - 1)^2*(Ln - 1)^2*Q^2)

' A family of curves is written by naming its members, so the load each one
' stands for has a name on the page rather than a number in a call.
fn light(f)   = gain(f, 0.25)
fn nominal(f) = gain(f, Q_nom)
fn heavy(f)   = gain(f, 0.85)
plot(light, nominal, heavy, f_sw_min, f_sw_max)
' image gain 480x240

' Unity at resonance, whatever the load — the property that makes f0 the design
' point. Below it the converter boosts, above it it bucks.
nominal(f0)
light(f0)
heavy(f0)

' Where the nominal curve peaks, and how much gain is there. The slope is exact
' rather than a difference quotient, so the peak is found by asking where the
' slope crosses zero.
fn slope(f) = derivative(nominal, f)
f_peak = roots(slope, f_min_res, f0) -> kHz
M_peak = nominal(f_peak)

' The gain the specification actually demands, at both ends of the input range.
V_bus_min = 340 V
V_bus_max = 420 V
M_needed_max = n*V_out/V_bus_min
M_needed_min = n*V_out/V_bus_max
margin = M_peak - M_needed_max
verdict = if margin > 0 then "gain margin available" else "redesign: peak gain too low"

' The impedance curve, drawn as a magnitude in ohms so the axis is a number.
fn Z_mag(f) = abs(Z_in(f))
fn Z_ohm(f) = Z_mag(f)/ohm
plot(Z_ohm, f_sw_min, f_sw_max)

' The lowest impedance across the operating range bounds the tank current, and
' `min` over a sampled vector is how a worksheet asks that without a loop.
sweep = range(f_sw_min, f_sw_max, 10 kHz)
Z_sweep = map(Z_ohm, sweep)
Z_low = min(Z_sweep)
I_tank_max = V_sq_rms/(Z_low ohm) -> A

' Currents and stresses, which is what the design is finally judged on.
I_pri = P_out/V_sq_rms
V_Cr_pk = I_pri*sqrt(2)*X_Cr(f0) -> V
E_Lr = 1/2*Lr*(I_pri*sqrt(2))^2 -> mJ

' Rated against the specification.
V_Cr_rating = 630 V
capacitor_ok = V_Cr_pk < V_Cr_rating

